Include email input and subscribe button, but it should be static only, no real submission or backend. It can show a tiny note: "Static demo form — no data is sent."

## 10. Footer
Include logo, short description, navigation links, fake social links, and copyright text.

# Content Style
- Confident, sharp, editorial, practical
- Avoid generic startup wording
- Make agriculture feel current and energetic
- Use realistic podcast episode titles and descriptions

# Interaction / Animation Requirements
- Animated waveform in hero or featured episode
- Hover effects on buttons and episode cards
- Scroll reveal or fade/slide-in sections
- Animated topic ticker/marquee
- Respect reduced motion if possible

# Deliverable
A complete static Astro website with reusable components where it makes sense. It should run locally without connecting to any API or service. Use mock data only. Please make the final result visually polished, responsive, and clearly designed as a brutalist agriculture podcast marketing site.

=== RL/code #2078 ===
Time-dependent geometry is currently difficult to use safely: the provider assumes callers pass time keys in insertion order, has no way to choose a discontinuous schedule, and malformed schedules can fail with incidental exceptions or silently become a static configuration. Add an explicit interpolation policy to the public geometry configuration and provider APIs while preserving existing callers.

The public `Geometry.from_dict` configuration must accept `geometry_interpolation_mode`, defaulting to `piecewise_linear`, with the values `piecewise_linear` and `step` (case-insensitive strings and the corresponding `InterpolationMode` enum are accepted where the provider API is called directly). For a time-dependent `geometry_configs` mapping, normalize slices by ascending finite time before constructing the provider without mutating the caller's mapping. Piecewise-linear mode interpolates every numeric geometry field between neighboring slices; step mode holds the slice at the greatest configured time less than or equal to the requested time. Requests outside the schedule use the first or last slice respectively.

Reject an empty schedule, non-finite time keys, or an unknown interpolation policy with `ValueError`. A time-dependent schedule must still reject slices with different geometry types or meshes, and an empty `geometry_configs` mapping must not silently fall back to a static default. Existing static geometry configuration and the default linear interpolation behavior must remain compatible.

=== RL/code #1838 ===
## Working with RGB(A) imagery is rough around the edges

I've been trying to do a pretty plain RGB workflow with datacube — read a 3-band GeoTIFF, peek at its geo info, do a tiny bit of processing, then write it back out as a COG — and I hit friction at every step. They all feel like the same underlying gap (RGB / RGBA imagery has an extra channel axis and nothing in the API treats it as a first-class case), so filing it as one report.

### 1. `rio_slurp_xarray` returns band-first

```python
da = rio_slurp_xarray('rgb.tif')
da.shape       # (3, H, W)
da.dims        # band axis first
```

This is how rasterio reads multi-band data, but for a 3- or 4-channel image (y, x, band) is what every imaging library I'd want to chain into expects — `matplotlib.imshow`, skimage, PIL, etc. So I end up sticking a transpose at every call site. Could the helper recognise the common RGB / RGBA case and hand me back image-shaped data by default?

### 2. `.geobox` on an image-shaped DataArray

If I take an RGB DataArray with axes laid out as (y, x, band) (either by transposing the result of (1), or by constructing one myself), accessing `.geobox` doesn't do the right thing — it seems to assume the last two axes are the spatial ones, so it ends up treating the channel axis as one of the spatial axes and either errors out or returns nonsense. I'd expect `.geobox` to find the actual y/x dims regardless of where the channel axis sits.

### 3. `write_cog` only accepts band-first 3D arrays

Symmetrically, on the write side:

```python
write_cog(rgb_yxc, 'out.tif')   # rgb_yxc has shape (H, W, 3)
```

doesn't work — `write_cog` treats a 3D array strictly as (band, y, x), so passing it the image-natural (y, x, band) layout either blows up on the shape check or writes garbled output. Same comment as (1): (y, x, band) is what comes out of most imaging pipelines, so it'd be great if `write_cog` accepted that layout too and figured out the right thing to write.

---

Could datacube handle RGB(A) imagery a bit more gracefully across these three spots? Happy to test against a sample image if useful.

