=== RL/code #1401 ===
# Problem Statement

I'm using python-opcua and I'm running into a modeling issue with audit events. The OPC UA spec lets the emitting node (the one actually firing the event) be different from the SourceNode the event describes — e.g. the Server object emits an AuditCreateSessionEvent whose SourceNode points at the session/session diagnostics node. But with `BaseEvent` and all the `Audit*Event` constructors I can only pass `sourcenode`, there's no way to tell it which node is actually emitting. Could we get a way to specify the emitting node separately when constructing these events, ideally defaulting to the Server object so I don't have to set it every time?

# Expected outcomes

- `BaseEvent` construction supports specifying an emitting node separately from `sourcenode`; when both are provided, the event’s emitting node behavior follows the emitting-node argument while the `SourceNode` event field still reflects `sourcenode`.
- `BaseEvent` construction remains backwards compatible for existing `sourcenode`, `message`, and `severity` usage.
- When no emitting node is provided to `BaseEvent`, the event defaults to being emitted by the OPC UA Server object.
- Public `Audit*Event` constructors support the same emitting-node option and pass it through consistently, so audit event subclasses can be emitted by a node different from the `SourceNode` they describe.
- Public `Audit*Event` constructors keep the same default behavior as `BaseEvent`: omitting the emitting node uses the Server object, and existing `sourcenode`, `message`, and `severity` arguments continue to work.

# Implementation notes

- Preserve the existing public event-construction style while adding the new emitting-node capability.
- The concrete internal representation, validation location, and propagation mechanism are up to the implementation, as long as externally constructed and generated events behave as described.
- Avoid coupling `SourceNode` semantics to the emitting node; callers must be able to set them independently.

=== RL/code #2535 ===
Django's migration machinery treats model fields as value objects: it deconstructs them into constructor arguments, stores that state, and clones fields while comparing model state. TaggableManager is a field-like relationship, but its deconstruction currently forgets relationship-only options. A project using a custom through model or a reverse relation name can therefore have a field that behaves one way at runtime and another way after a migration state is reconstructed; repeated schema checks can also see a false difference because cloning silently falls back to the built-in generic through model.

Make TaggableManager's public deconstruct() and clone() behavior migration-safe and consistent with Django's field conventions. A custom through reference supplied as a dotted string must be emitted unchanged. When a through model class is supplied, emit its stable app-label/model-name reference, just as Django relationship fields do; after a lazy string reference has resolved to a model class, deconstruction must still emit that same reference. Include related_name whenever it was explicitly supplied, including an empty string, and omit it when it is unset. Preserve blank=True and the existing verbose_name/help_text values, while continuing to omit the runtime-only serialize and null constructor flags.

Calling clone() must recreate an equivalent manager: its through reference, reverse-name setting, blank flag, and presentation metadata must survive, and the clone must be a separate field whose relationship metadata can be changed without changing the original. Keep the default TaggableManager compact: an unconfigured manager should continue omitting through, related_name, and blank from deconstruction and a clone should use the built-in TaggedItem through model. The behavior must work both before and after Django contributes/resolves the field; do not require callers to edit generated migrations manually.

